    description: "FOUND 2026-10-03 while fixing WI-vinuh-tizad-lovum-bikir-fuzid-lovun-januz-nupig (branch jgstern-agent/fix/vinuh).\n\ngo.py's NewXxx constructor convention (_type_from_constructor_call) types `x.NewFoo()` as `x.Foo` whenever the operand is a bare identifier, without asking whether `x` is an imported PACKAGE or a local VALUE. Its docstring says \"Only a BARE identifier operand can be a package name\", and that is the whole check. In the binding typer the convention runs BEFORE the return-type registry, so a method whose DECLARED result differs from its name loses to the guess.\n\nLive repro (dev 604c91e3f2 and the vinuh branch alike; the binding typer is unchanged there, only refactored):\n    type Engine struct{}; type Result struct{}; type Query struct{}\n    func (e *Engine) NewQuery() *Result\n    func (r *Result) Rows() {}; func (q *Query) Rows() {}\n    func Use(e *Engine)      { q := e.NewQuery(); q.Rows() }   -> Query.Rows   (WRONG, typed_receiver)\n    func UseChain(e *Engine) { e.NewQuery().Rows() }           -> Result.Rows  (right, chained_return_type via WI-vadin)\nThe two spellings of one call disagree, and the binding form is a confident wrong edge.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not done): the convention applies to a package-qualified callee only when the operand is in import_aliases (the binding typer would need the file's aliases), or the registry's declared answer goes first for a typed receiver. NOT MEASURED: corpus frequency (a method named NewXxx returning a type other than Xxx)."  # 51d1
    fields: {}  # 51d1
